{"step_index":317,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-01T06:51:18Z","thinking":"**Developing Animated Concepts**\n\nI'm working on an animated map. The goal is to visually represent a flight from Washington D.C. to New Delhi, India. I'm also thinking of including a slower-moving vehicle, perhaps a car or ship, to highlight the speed difference between air and ground/sea travel. This should create an interesting visual contrast!\n\n\n**Planning Animation Steps**\n\nI've decided to download a high-resolution world map PNG directly using a script if it's not already present. I found a suitable equirectangular map URL. The next step involves mapping longitude/latitude to X/Y coordinates for the airplane's path, starting in Washington D.C. (roughly -77, 38) and ending in New Delhi (approximately 77, 28). I'll also figure out the second vehicle's slower path.\n\n\n**Comparing Travel Times**\n\nI'm now focusing on contrasting the plane's speed with a slower vehicle. I've decided on a ship traveling from New York to London to highlight the difference. The plane will fly from Washington D.C. to New Delhi. The plane's flight will be 15 seconds, and the ship will move a comparably short distance. I've broken the animation into five agents, each handling a 90-frame chunk, to accelerate the process.\n\n\n**Downloading Map Image**\n\nI've decided to download the map image directly using `urllib.request` in Python, saving it to a specified path. I will make the master script to download the image. Now I have a 1920x1080 resolution world map, perfect for this animation. The shortest path will be a Bezier curve to make the path look cool. I will spawn five agents and ensure it does not timeout!\n\n\n**Implementing Animation Path**\n\nI'm now focusing on the visual representation, specifically the paths. I've calculated the control point for the Bezier curve of the plane's flight, making it arc upwards. I will represent the plane with a tr